## How authentication works

The browser never sees a token.

```
form post ──▶ +page.server.ts action  (same origin)
                    │  calls the API, writes an httpOnly cookie
                    ▼
               your API
```

Sign-in and sign-up are **form actions**, not API routes — the SvelteKit way.
They work with JavaScript disabled, and `use:enhance` upgrades them to a
fetch-based submit when it is available.

| Route | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `POST /login` | Exchange credentials for a session |
| `POST /register` | Create an account and start a session |
| `POST /logout` | Revoke the session and clear the cookies |

Tokens live in httpOnly, `sameSite=lax` cookies that JavaScript cannot read.
`src/hooks.server.ts` reads them once per request onto `event.locals`, and
anything under `$lib/server` is refused by SvelteKit if a component tries to
import it — so a token cannot leak into the client bundle by accident.

The hook also redirects signed-out visitors away from `/dashboard`. That is a
convenience: the page's own `load` re-checks the session, and the API remains
the authority.
<% } -%>

## Project structure

```
src/
<% if (it.hasAuth) { -%>
├── hooks.server.ts       session per request, route protection
<% } -%>
├── lib/
│   ├── server/           never bundled into client code
│   └── components/       button, fields, modal, toast, states
└── routes/               pages and their server actions
```
<% if (it.hasDocker) { -%>

## Docker

```bash
docker compose up --build
```

The image runs the Node adapter output as a non-root user. Set `ORIGIN` to the
public URL: SvelteKit rejects form posts whose origin it cannot verify, and a
mismatch there is the usual cause of a 403 on submit.
<% } -%>

